WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.057
Memory safety is stronger when the
program's types represent reality accurately.

2
00:00:04.137 --> 00:00:07.338
Rust lets developers move checks closer to
the point where data is created,

3
00:00:07.418 --> 00:00:08.993
parsed, or accepted from a boundary.

4
00:00:09.073 --> 00:00:10.903
Instead of letting raw strings, integers,

5
00:00:10.983 --> 00:00:13.320
or loosely related flags spread through
the codebase,

6
00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:15.534
teams can create types that carry domain
meaning.

7
00:00:15.614 --> 00:00:19.070
Newtypes are useful when two values share
the same primitive representation but

8
00:00:19.150 --> 00:00:20.522
should not be interchangeable.

9
00:00:20.602 --> 00:00:22.787
A user identifier, tenant identifier, path
label,

10
00:00:22.867 --> 00:00:25.001
and request identifier may all look like
strings,

11
00:00:25.081 --> 00:00:28.790
but they may follow different validation
rules and carry different security meaning.

12
00:00:28.870 --> 00:00:32.529
Giving them separate types reduces
accidental mixups and makes interfaces clearer.

13
00:00:32.609 --> 00:00:36.217
Enums can model states and transitions
without relying on scattered boolean flags.

14
00:00:36.297 --> 00:00:39.295
A connection, job, or parser may have a
small set of meaningful states.

15
00:00:39.375 --> 00:00:43.033
Encoding those states directly makes
impossible combinations harder to represent.

16
00:00:43.113 --> 00:00:46.721
Option represents absence explicitly, and
Result represents failure explicitly,

17
00:00:46.801 --> 00:00:50.104
so downstream code must acknowledge those
cases instead of relying on hidden

18
00:00:50.184 --> 00:00:50.794
assumptions.

19
00:00:50.874 --> 00:00:54.329
Constructors and parsers can validate data
once and return a type that later code

20
00:00:54.409 --> 00:00:54.866
can trust.

21
00:00:54.946 --> 00:00:57.080
This reduces repeated checks, lowers
ambiguity,

22
00:00:57.160 --> 00:00:59.040
and makes invalid states harder to
express.

23
00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:02.576
Type-driven design does not replace
validation, but it gives validation a home.

24
00:01:02.656 --> 00:01:05.857
The result is downstream code that is
simpler, safer, and easier to review.

25
00:01:05.937 --> 00:01:07.716
This pattern also improves maintenance.

26
00:01:07.796 --> 00:01:09.015
When a requirement changes,

27
00:01:09.095 --> 00:01:12.296
reviewers can look for the type or
constructor that owns the rule instead of

28
00:01:12.376 --> 00:01:14.104
searching for scattered conditionals.

29
00:01:14.184 --> 00:01:17.639
Memory safety and correctness both benefit
when the codebase has obvious places

30
00:01:17.719 --> 00:01:20.260
where assumptions are created, enforced,
and documented.
